1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:10,387
It isn't right, and Git didn't lie. git
blame labels every line of a file with the

2
00:00:10,427 --> 00:00:16,458
commit that last changed it. A commit is
one saved snapshot of the project, with its

3
00:00:16,498 --> 00:00:22,313
author. On Friday someone ran a formatter,
a tool that tidies the layout of code.

4
00:00:22,653 --> 00:00:27,074
Every line whose indentation or quotes
changed now carries that commit and that

5
00:00:27,114 --> 00:00:31,592
name. The person who wrote the logic, the
person who introduced the bug, and the

6
00:00:31,632 --> 00:00:36,336
reason for either are one step further back,
and plain blame doesn't take that step.

7
00:00:36,676 --> 00:00:39,247
There's a second way blame
misleads, and it's worse.

8
00:00:39,287 --> 00:00:42,260
The line that caused the regression
in scorekit was deleted.

9
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:45,876
Blame annotates lines that exist. It
has no row for a line that is gone.

10
00:00:45,916 --> 00:00:49,844
Keep that Friday formatter in mind. By
the end you'll look straight through it.

11
00:00:51,684 --> 00:00:55,153
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:55,193 --> 00:00:59,697
In the previous video you searched history
for content: a string, a function, a

13
00:00:59,737 --> 00:01:04,299
deleted file. Today you look at the tool
most engineers reach for first, and you

14
00:01:04,339 --> 00:01:09,074
learn to state exactly what it claims. Then
you put all the tools of this module in

15
00:01:09,114 --> 00:01:13,848
order: a method that takes you from a symptom
to the commit, to a confirmation, and

16
00:01:13,888 --> 00:01:16,149
to the list of releases that contain it.

17
00:01:16,489 --> 00:01:21,567
One note on the method. The curriculum
summary for this video calls it a seven-step

18
00:01:21,607 --> 00:01:26,623
method. The textbook's table in section
14A.19 has nine steps, and the textbook is

19
00:01:26,663 --> 00:01:28,720
the authority, so you'll see nine.

20
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:32,464
state precisely what one
row of git blame asserts;

21
00:01:32,804 --> 00:01:37,433
look through a reformatting commit with
-w, --ignore-rev and an ignore-revs file;

22
00:01:37,773 --> 00:01:40,470
follow moved and copied
code with -M and -C;

23
00:01:40,810 --> 00:01:43,577
explain what blame shows
after a squash merge;

24
00:01:43,917 --> 00:01:45,045
apply the method from
symptom to blast radius.

25
00:01:46,885 --> 00:01:51,736
In one sentence: 🟢 SAFE, git blame annotates
every line of a file, as it is in one

26
00:01:51,776 --> 00:01:54,957
revision, with the commit
that last changed that line.

27
00:01:54,997 --> 00:01:57,224
A revision is any commit you can name.

28
00:01:57,564 --> 00:02:03,553
Precisely. Blame starts from
the file in the given revision.

29
00:02:03,593 --> 00:02:11,793
That's HEAD by default, the commit you're
on, or the working tree file, the one on

30
00:02:11,833 --> 00:02:19,832
your disk, if it has uncommitted changes,
which are attributed to "Not Committed

31
00:02:19,872 --> 00:02:20,334
Yet".

32
00:02:20,674 --> 00:02:24,991
It compares the file with the same file
in the parent commit, the commit this one

33
00:02:25,031 --> 00:02:29,186
was built on. Lines that the diff shows
as unchanged are passed to the parent.

34
00:02:29,226 --> 00:02:31,875
Lines that it shows as
added stay with the commit.

35
00:02:32,215 --> 00:02:37,055
The parent is then treated the same
way, until every line has an owner.

36
00:02:37,095 --> 00:02:42,691
The manual adds two sentences you should
be able to quote: "The origin of lines is

37
00:02:42,731 --> 00:02:48,464
automatically followed across whole-file
renames", and "the report does not tell you

38
00:02:48,504 --> 00:02:52,450
anything about lines which have
been deleted or replaced".

39
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:57,681
Inside .git, nothing is written. The work
is one diff per commit that touched the

40
00:02:57,721 --> 00:03:02,552
file, so blame on an old, busy file in a
large repository takes noticeable time.

41
00:03:02,892 --> 00:03:07,576
So what does a row assert? Try it now,
on paper, for thirty seconds: write one

42
00:03:07,616 --> 00:03:11,210
sentence that starts "this commit
is". Then say your answer.

43
00:03:16,050 --> 00:03:20,821
Here's the exact version. This commit is
the last one whose diff against its parent

44
00:03:20,861 --> 00:03:24,472
shows this line as added. Nothing
about who designed the logic.

45
00:03:24,512 --> 00:03:27,718
Nothing about why. Nothing
about what stood here before.

46
00:03:28,058 --> 00:03:32,459
You want to know who wrote this logic.
Blame gives who last changed the line,

47
00:03:32,499 --> 00:03:37,074
possibly a formatter or a rename of a
variable. You want to know who removed the

48
00:03:37,114 --> 00:03:41,745
line that used to be here. Blame gives
nothing, and you use git log -S or git log

49
00:03:41,785 --> 00:03:41,918
-L.

50
00:03:42,258 --> 00:03:46,707
You want to know why. Blame gives an ID,
and git show gives the message and the

51
00:03:46,747 --> 00:03:51,138
whole commit. You want to know whom to
ask about code that arrived in a squash

52
00:03:51,178 --> 00:03:55,853
merge. Blame gives the person who ran the
merge, and you read the trailers, the key

53
00:03:55,893 --> 00:04:00,625
and value lines that end a commit message,
or the pull request, GitHub's proposal to

54
00:04:00,665 --> 00:04:01,591
merge the branch.

55
00:04:01,931 --> 00:04:07,081
You want to know whether the line is the
bug. Blame gives nothing, because the

56
00:04:07,121 --> 00:04:12,403
defect may be a line that is missing or
one in another file, and that's what git

57
00:04:12,443 --> 00:04:16,595
bisect is for: a binary search
over commits, in the next video.

58
00:04:16,935 --> 00:04:21,062
Three refinements deal with the three ways
the last change can be uninteresting.

59
00:04:21,402 --> 00:04:26,251
A reformatting commit. -w ignores whitespace
when blame compares a commit with its

60
00:04:26,291 --> 00:04:31,198
parent. --ignore-rev goes further: it removes
one commit from consideration, "as if

61
00:04:31,238 --> 00:04:35,788
the change never happened", and blames
its lines "on the previous commit that

62
00:04:35,828 --> 00:04:40,080
changed that line or nearby lines".
A file of full object IDs, read with

63
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:44,790
--ignore-revs-file or named by
blame.ignoreRevsFile, makes that permanent for a

64
00:04:44,830 --> 00:04:45,445
repository.

65
00:04:45,785 --> 00:04:52,647
Moved code. -M looks for the origin of a
line in other places of the same file.

66
00:04:52,687 --> 00:04:59,898
-C looks in files modified in the same
commit. A second -C also looks in every file

67
00:04:59,938 --> 00:05:04,703
of the parent, for commits
that create the blamed file.

68
00:05:04,743 --> 00:05:09,858
A third looks in every file of
the parent, in every commit.

69
00:05:09,898 --> 00:05:17,109
Each rung costs more. And there are minimums:
20 alphanumeric characters for -M, 40

70
00:05:17,149 --> 00:05:17,720
for -C.

71
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,934
A squash merge. It records the content
of a branch as one new commit.

72
00:05:21,974 --> 00:05:25,564
Blame on the target branch then
knows one commit and one author.

73
00:05:25,904 --> 00:05:29,640
When not to rely on the ignore
list: its attribution is a guess.

74
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:34,478
Git maps an ignored line to the line of
the parent that looks like its origin, and

75
00:05:34,518 --> 00:05:38,726
for a line that the ignored commit
really introduced there is no origin.

76
00:05:38,766 --> 00:05:43,447
And list only commits that change no
behavior: formatter runs, mass renames of a

77
00:05:43,487 --> 00:05:48,344
symbol, license header updates. Listing a
commit that also changed logic hides that

78
00:05:48,384 --> 00:05:49,347
logic from blame.

79
00:05:51,187 --> 00:05:55,885
Now a picture to keep. The textbook's
analogy: a "last edited by" column beside a

80
00:05:55,925 --> 00:06:00,566
document. The column is reliable about
the last edit and silent about everything

81
00:06:00,606 --> 00:06:05,246
else: the edit before it, the paragraph
that used to stand here, and whether the

82
00:06:05,286 --> 00:06:08,581
last editor wrote the sentence
or only fixed its spacing.

83
00:06:08,921 --> 00:06:12,913
The analogy breaks on the word "line". A
document editor knows which sentence you

84
00:06:12,953 --> 00:06:16,896
touched. Git doesn't. It decides which
old line corresponds to which new line by

85
00:06:16,936 --> 00:06:20,480
running a diff, so the attribution
depends on how the diff aligned them.

86
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:24,155
The working rule that follows: treat a blame
row as a pointer into history, never as

87
00:06:24,195 --> 00:06:27,530
a verdict about a person. The next command
after git blame is git show with that ID.

88
00:06:29,370 --> 00:06:34,855
One line, line 7 of scorekit/text.py,
and four commits of its history.

89
00:06:35,195 --> 00:06:38,211
Plain blame stops at the
newest commit, the formatter.

90
00:06:38,552 --> 00:06:42,103
With --ignore-rev for the formatter, it
stops at d926d3c, where the line was

91
00:06:42,143 --> 00:06:44,041
replaced and the lowercasing disappeared.

92
00:06:44,381 --> 00:06:48,957
If you blame the parent of that commit,
you're asking about an older line 7, and you

93
00:06:48,997 --> 00:06:52,090
stop at the commit that moved
the function into the file.

94
00:06:52,430 --> 00:06:57,194
With -C at that commit, blame crosses
into the file the function came from.

95
00:06:57,534 --> 00:07:02,011
Four marks, four different answers to "who
wrote line 7", and every one of them is

96
00:07:02,051 --> 00:07:04,214
correct for the question that was asked.

97
00:07:06,054 --> 00:07:11,994
Read one row out loud: the abbreviated commit
ID, the author and author date of that

98
00:07:12,034 --> 00:07:15,909
commit, the line number in
this revision, and the line.

99
00:07:15,949 --> 00:07:21,888
-s drops author and date, and -L limits the
output with the same range syntax as git

100
00:07:21,928 --> 00:07:22,387
log -L.

101
00:07:22,727 --> 00:07:26,488
A revision before the path asks
about an older state of the file.

102
00:07:26,828 --> 00:07:31,743
Point at the extra column, scorer.py. It
appears whenever a line comes from a commit

103
00:07:31,783 --> 00:07:36,344
in which the file had another name. A
whole-file rename doesn't disturb blame.

104
00:07:36,684 --> 00:07:38,786
For scripts there is a porcelain format.

105
00:07:39,126 --> 00:07:46,689
Full IDs and one header per attribution.
Find the field named previous: the commit

106
00:07:46,729 --> 00:07:52,530
before the blamed one. That's the
key to stepping further back.

107
00:07:52,870 --> 00:07:58,402
Now the limit that matters for our regression.
Predict: line 7 used to lowercase the

108
00:07:58,442 --> 00:08:02,713
text. What does blame say about
that line today? Say it out loud.

109
00:08:02,753 --> 00:08:03,376
I'll wait.

110
00:08:08,216 --> 00:08:12,111
Before d926d3c, line 7 contained .lower().
Today a different line 7 exists, and

111
00:08:12,151 --> 00:08:15,497
blame reports on that one. There's
no row for the line that is gone.

112
00:08:15,537 --> 00:08:17,489
That's the second trap from the opening.

113
00:08:17,829 --> 00:08:22,835
Five of seven lines carry 9c8df982.
Quick quiz, three options.

114
00:08:22,875 --> 00:08:29,021
With -w, do all five fall through to an
older commit, four of them, or none?

115
00:08:29,061 --> 00:08:30,324
Say it out loud.

116
00:08:35,164 --> 00:08:43,204
Four. The lines that were only
re-indented fall through to b4b066b4.

117
00:08:43,244 --> 00:08:52,117
Line 13 stays with the formatter, because
there it also changed the quotes.

118
00:08:52,157 --> 00:08:56,157
Whitespace wasn't the only change.

119
00:08:56,497 --> 00:09:01,599
Now line 13 goes back too. That's the
Friday formatter from the opening, looked

120
00:09:01,639 --> 00:09:07,001
through. Typing the option every time doesn't
scale, so the IDs go into a file: one

121
00:09:07,041 --> 00:09:10,711
full object ID per line, with
comments after a hash sign.

122
00:09:18,051 --> 00:09:23,523
Point at the question mark on line 3.
blame.markIgnoredLines flags every line whose

123
00:09:23,563 --> 00:09:29,036
attribution passed through an ignored commit.
Keep that mark: the attribution of an

124
00:09:29,076 --> 00:09:30,630
ignored line is a guess.

125
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:36,528
Two failures are common enough to show.
Almost everyone meets the first one.

126
00:09:36,868 --> 00:09:42,105
The file must contain full IDs. And once
the key is set, the file must exist in

127
00:09:42,145 --> 00:09:43,642
every commit you visit.

128
00:09:43,982 --> 00:09:48,016
The tag, a fixed name for an older commit,
predates the file, and blame fails.

129
00:09:48,056 --> 00:09:50,994
An empty --ignore-revs-file
clears the list of revisions.

130
00:09:51,034 --> 00:09:53,762
It doesn't stop Git from
opening the configured file.

131
00:09:53,802 --> 00:09:58,150
The manual documents a prefix for path-valued
settings, :(optional): the variable is

132
00:09:58,190 --> 00:10:01,545
treated as if it doesn't exist
when the named path doesn't exist.

133
00:10:01,885 --> 00:10:07,927
At v0.2.0 blame runs and nothing is ignored.
Back on main the list applies again.

134
00:10:08,267 --> 00:10:13,743
Every line belongs to the commit that
moved it. Now today's file, with -C, and a

135
00:10:13,783 --> 00:10:19,053
prediction: -C wants 40 alphanumeric
characters. Are there 40 left unchanged?

136
00:10:19,393 --> 00:10:24,269
No. After later edits only short fragments
of the moved block survive, and the

137
00:10:24,309 --> 00:10:29,500
default threshold rejects them. With a lower
one, given as -C5, line 6 goes back to

138
00:10:29,540 --> 00:10:34,101
b2502383 in scorer.py. That's why moved
code is sometimes not recognized.

139
00:10:34,441 --> 00:10:37,222
The squash merge.

140
00:10:37,562 --> 00:10:40,770
On main, the person who
squashed "wrote" the function.

141
00:10:40,810 --> 00:10:44,138
The branch, while it still
exists, has the real history.

142
00:10:44,178 --> 00:10:46,244
Nothing links the two in the graph.

143
00:10:46,584 --> 00:10:50,274
After the branch is deleted, the Co-authored-by
trailers and the pull request are

144
00:10:50,314 --> 00:10:53,728
the only record of who wrote which part.
That's a real cost of squash-merge

145
00:10:53,768 --> 00:10:56,583
workflows, to be weighed against
the linear history they give.

146
00:10:56,623 --> 00:10:59,991
The opposite question, "which merge
put this line on main", is answered by

147
00:11:00,031 --> 00:11:01,971
--first-parent: blame
then stops at merges.

148
00:11:08,811 --> 00:11:13,468
Steps 3 to 5 are fast and need
an idea of where the fault is.

149
00:11:13,508 --> 00:11:18,550
Step 6, bisect, is slower and needs
no idea at all, only the test.

150
00:11:18,590 --> 00:11:24,403
Step 7 isn't optional: every tool before
it produces a suspect, not a proof.

151
00:11:24,743 --> 00:11:26,279
The symptom, and the code
that produces the behavior.

152
00:11:26,619 --> 00:11:28,548
The documentation says
"equal after normalization".

153
00:11:28,588 --> 00:11:29,590
That points at normalize().

154
00:11:29,930 --> 00:11:33,348
Both body lines are owned by the
formatter commit. Look through it.

155
00:11:33,688 --> 00:11:41,651
One line was last changed for a real
reason, by d926d3c, the day before the

156
00:11:41,691 --> 00:11:45,066
formatter ran. Read that commit.

157
00:11:48,406 --> 00:11:51,290
The message promises speed
and no change of behavior.

158
00:11:51,330 --> 00:11:55,924
The patch replaces the lowercasing call with
a pattern substitution, and .lower() is

159
00:11:55,964 --> 00:12:00,338
gone, while the documentation still says
that normalization lowercases the text.

160
00:12:00,378 --> 00:12:04,090
The pickaxe confirms that this is
where the call left the code base.

161
00:12:04,430 --> 00:12:09,968
Step 7. Test on both sides of the suspect,
in two temporary worktrees, extra working

162
00:12:10,008 --> 00:12:15,281
directories of the same repository, so
that the main working tree isn't touched.

163
00:12:15,321 --> 00:12:20,793
git worktree add is 🟢 SAFE, and worktrees
have their own videos later in this part.

164
00:12:20,833 --> 00:12:22,386
Predict the two results.

165
00:12:22,726 --> 00:12:25,835
True before, False after. That's
the proof. Step 8, the spread.

166
00:12:31,675 --> 00:12:35,652
The answer for the CTO: the
harness regressed, not the model.

167
00:12:35,692 --> 00:12:40,920
Commit d926d3c, on Thursday 10 September,
dropped lowercasing from normalization

168
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:46,254
while optimizing it. Release v0.2.0 contains
the commit, and so does every branch

169
00:12:46,294 --> 00:12:50,863
cut since. The fix is one line, plus
a test with a capitalized answer.

170
00:12:50,903 --> 00:12:55,934
Notice how that sentence is phrased: it
names a commit and a mechanism, not a

171
00:12:55,974 --> 00:12:56,461
culprit.

172
00:12:58,301 --> 00:12:59,949
Five mistakes to watch for.

173
00:13:00,289 --> 00:13:04,487
Reading a blame row as authorship of the
logic. Root cause: a row names the last

174
00:13:04,527 --> 00:13:08,830
commit whose diff shows the line as added,
which may be a formatter, a rename of a

175
00:13:08,870 --> 00:13:09,943
variable or a squash.

176
00:13:10,283 --> 00:13:13,692
Looking in blame for who removed something.
Root cause: blame annotates lines that

177
00:13:13,732 --> 00:13:16,047
exist in the blamed revision;
a deleted line has no row.

178
00:13:16,387 --> 00:13:27,543
Short IDs in the ignore-revs file. Root
cause: the file is a list of full object

179
00:13:27,583 --> 00:13:36,360
IDs, and Git rejects an abbreviation
as an invalid object name.

180
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:39,821
Blame fails on an old commit after
blame.ignoreRevsFile is set.

181
00:13:39,861 --> 00:13:44,034
Root cause: the configured file must exist
in the checked-out state; the :(optional)

182
00:13:44,074 --> 00:13:46,342
prefix makes the setting
tolerate its absence.

183
00:13:46,682 --> 00:13:49,799
Listing a commit before it
reached the shared branch.

184
00:13:49,839 --> 00:13:54,505
Root cause: rebasing or squashing the
listed commit changes its ID and silently

185
00:13:54,545 --> 00:13:55,577
empties the entry.

186
00:13:57,417 --> 00:14:02,127
Now, out of the lab. A backend team adopts
a formatter across a Java and Python

187
00:14:02,167 --> 00:14:06,936
monorepo. The formatter commit touches
most files. From that day, the blame view

188
00:14:06,976 --> 00:14:11,806
names one engineer on almost every line,
and code review comments begin with "you

189
00:14:11,846 --> 00:14:12,528
wrote this".

190
00:14:12,868 --> 00:14:18,744
The team does three things, in this order.
They wait until the formatter commit is

191
00:14:18,784 --> 00:14:24,732
on main, so its ID is final. They commit a
file named .git-blame-ignore-revs in the

192
00:14:24,772 --> 00:14:29,565
root, with the full ID and a comment
that says what the commit was.

193
00:14:29,605 --> 00:14:35,337
And they add the configuration key to the
onboarding script with the :(optional)

194
00:14:35,377 --> 00:14:40,387
prefix, because engineers bisect
across commits that predate the file.

195
00:14:40,427 --> 00:14:46,087
They also turn on the mark for ignored
lines, so that nobody mistakes a guessed

196
00:14:46,127 --> 00:14:47,746
attribution for a fact.

197
00:14:49,586 --> 00:14:55,717
Your turn. Do Lab 11.6, "Blame through a
reformatting commit with an ignore-revs

198
00:14:55,757 --> 00:14:59,266
file", in
lab-manual/m11-history-forensics.md.

199
00:14:59,606 --> 00:15:02,984
Before you run blame with the ignore list,
predict for each line of the function

200
00:15:03,024 --> 00:15:06,487
which commit it will be attributed to, and
which lines will carry a question mark.

201
00:15:06,527 --> 00:15:10,033
Then make the lab's failure happen on purpose
and predict the error text before you

202
00:15:10,073 --> 00:15:10,375
read it.

203
00:15:10,715 --> 00:15:16,856
The challenge is Exercise 11.3, Level 1,
"Blame, and one step further back", in

204
00:15:16,896 --> 00:15:20,299
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

205
00:15:22,139 --> 00:15:23,406
Pause and answer out loud.

206
00:15:28,246 --> 00:15:31,926
A strong answer opens with a definition
that is narrow and exact: one revision, one

207
00:15:31,966 --> 00:15:35,063
line as it stands, the last commit
that changed it, decided by a diff.

208
00:15:35,103 --> 00:15:38,559
It then gives three situations that are
different in kind, not three formatter

209
00:15:38,599 --> 00:15:42,189
stories, and for each one names the tool
or option that recovers the real origin.

210
00:15:42,229 --> 00:15:45,954
It mentions what blame can't show at all.
And it ends with the practice that follows

211
00:15:45,994 --> 00:15:49,315
from the definition: what you run next,
and how you speak about the result.

212
00:15:51,155 --> 00:15:54,082
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

213
00:15:54,422 --> 00:16:00,396
A blame row names the last commit that
changed a line as it stands in one revision;

214
00:16:00,436 --> 00:16:03,004
it is a pointer to a commit to read.

215
00:16:03,344 --> 00:16:07,891
-w and --ignore-rev look through reformatting;
an ignore-revs file holds full IDs,

216
00:16:07,931 --> 00:16:10,520
and its attributions are
guesses worth marking.

217
00:16:10,860 --> 00:16:16,271
-M and -C follow moved and copied code at
increasing cost and with minimum sizes.

218
00:16:16,611 --> 00:16:19,241
After a squash merge, blame on the target
branch knows one commit and one author.

219
00:16:19,581 --> 00:16:24,625
Every investigative tool produces a
suspect; the test at the suspect and at its

220
00:16:24,665 --> 00:16:25,912
parent is the proof.

221
00:16:27,752 --> 00:16:32,830
Read sections 14A.16 to
14A.19 of Chapter 14A.

222
00:16:33,170 --> 00:16:41,255
Today you learned to read a blame row as a
pointer and not a verdict, and you walked

223
00:16:41,295 --> 00:16:49,091
from a symptom to a proven commit. Try the
ignore-revs lab before the next video.

224
00:16:49,131 --> 00:16:56,830
Next time: git bisect, binary search over
commits. Until then, look at the state

225
00:16:56,870 --> 00:17:01,376
first and type second.
See you in the next one.
